Id: no-server-type-in-a-test
Pattern: ^using (MediaBrowser|Jellyfin\.Data|Jellyfin\.Database)
Subject: *Tests/*Tests.cs
Except: :!*SurfaceAdapterTests.cs :!*ServerLibraryAdapterTests.cs :!*RefreshTaskTests.cs :!*PluginServiceRegistratorTests.cs :!*PackagedPluginIdentityTests.cs
Issue: 49

A test that constructs a server type drags in the server's assembly graph, and a
test that needs a database or a library manager is a test somebody deletes. The
way out is that the plugin talks to the server through interfaces of its own and
the tests talk to hand-written fakes of those, which is what #49 asks for. The
payoff is not speed: a fake can count the calls made to it, and several
done-whens in this plan are written as counts, so a test that reaches past the
interface is a test that cannot make the assertion it was written for.

The pattern reads the import line rather than a type name, because the set of
server types is not enumerable and the namespace is what every one of them
arrives through. Anchoring at the start of the line is what makes it an import
rather than a mention: a sentence in a remark naming `MediaBrowser` is a file
talking about the server, and a file talking about the server is not a file
reaching into it. `Jellyfin.Data` and `Jellyfin.Database` are beside
`MediaBrowser` because the server's own types live under all three and a rule
naming one of them would move the reach rather than refuse it.

THE SUBJECT IS A TEST FILE AND THAT IS THE WHOLE OF WHY THE EXCEPTIONS ARE THE
FEW BELOW AND NOT ONE PER FAKE. A fake standing in for a server interface has to name the type it
implements; that is its job, and it is #49's own first condition. A rule
excepting each fake by name would be a list of today's fakes, and the list grows
every time the plugin talks to one more of the server, silently, with nothing
red. So the fakes are outside the subject rather than exempted inside it: this
lint's suite names a test file `<something>Tests.cs` and names apparatus after
what it does, and the subject is written to that convention. What keeps the
convention load bearing is `no-test-outside-a-tests-file`, without which a test
could be written into a file this subject does not reach and the property would
be silently narrower than its prose.

THE EXCEPTIONS ARE THE SEAMS TO THE SERVER, NOT THE FILES THAT ARE RED TODAY.
The files in the plugin that speak the server's vocabulary do so because that is
where it has to be spoken - the surface adapter, the library adapter, the
scheduled task, the plugin class and the registrator - and the tests of those
have to speak it to call them at all. The library adapter is the one that
arrived after this paragraph was first written: #89 asks the refresh to leave
out titles the server already holds, and that question reaches the server's own
library. #49's condition writes that carve-out as "the adapter's own tests",
which was the whole of the seam on the day it was written and is not any more,
and this rule is the condition read against the tree rather than against its own
wording. The count is deliberately not stated here, because the last time it was
the tree grew a seam and the sentence did not.
`no-channel-type-outside-surface` already excepts the same adapters
for the same reason. A test file outside them that has to be renamed to pass is
the rule telling its author it is on the wrong side of the boundary, which is the
sentence that neighbour already carries.

Its bound is the one every rule here has, and one more. A line at a time: a
server type reached through a fully qualified name written inside a method body
carries no import for this to match, and a global using in a project file would
put the import somewhere this subject does not read. What holds those is the
same thing that holds the whole boundary in what is built rather than in what is
written - `SuiteAssemblyReferencesTests` refuses any assembly the allow-list
beside it does not name - and this rule is the earlier and cheaper of the two
rather than a replacement for it.
